# Rebuilding your server from a backup

This file is part of every backup snapshot. If you are reading it after
extracting a snapshot, everything described below is already in your
hands.

You do not need our software, our tools, or our help to get your data
back. You need three ordinary programs: `restic` (reads the backup),
`docker` (runs the applications), and `psql` (loads the databases).

## 1. What is in the snapshot

| What | Where in the snapshot |
| --- | --- |
| Application data (files, uploads, mailboxes) | `{{ storage_mount_point }}/docker/volumes/<name>/_data/` |
| Application definitions (the compose file for each app) | `{{ storage_mount_point }}/docker/volumes/{{ portainer_data_volume | default('catena-portainer-data') }}/_data/compose/<id>/` |
| Database exports (one file per database, plain SQL) | `{{ storage_mount_point }}/backup-staging/pg/*.sql.gz` |
| Files stored outside a volume | `{{ storage_mount_point }}/apps/` |
| Your settings and passwords | `/etc/catena/config.json` |
| The software version this server ran | `/etc/catena/version.txt` |

Everything is ordinary files and directories. Nothing is in a proprietary
format, and nothing requires our software to open.

## 2. What is deliberately NOT in the snapshot, and why

- **Application images.** These are downloaded fresh from their public
  sources. Storing them would multiply the size of every backup for no
  benefit, since the exact versions are recorded in each compose file.
- **Monitoring and antivirus history** (Gatus, Healthchecks, Beszel,
  ClamAV signatures). These rebuild themselves within minutes and change
  constantly, which would inflate every backup.
- **The server's own SSH identity keys.** A rebuilt server generates its
  own. Carrying the old ones forward would be a security problem, not a
  convenience.

## 3. Read the software version first

```
restic dump latest /etc/catena/version.txt
```

The first line is the exact version of the installation software that
built this server. If you are rebuilding a full server rather than a
single application, install that same version before restoring. Later
versions may expect a newer database format than your backup contains.

## 4. Restore one application by hand

Extract just what that application needs:

```
restic restore latest --target /recovered \
    --include '{{ storage_mount_point }}/docker/volumes/<volume-name>' \
    --include '{{ storage_mount_point }}/docker/volumes/{{ portainer_data_volume | default('catena-portainer-data') }}/_data/compose'
```

Find the application's definition under the extracted `compose/`
directory. Each subdirectory holds one application's compose file, and
its settings alongside.

Copy the data into place and start it:

```
docker volume create <volume-name>
cp -a /recovered{{ storage_mount_point }}/docker/volumes/<volume-name>/_data/. \
      /var/lib/docker/volumes/<volume-name>/_data/
docker compose -f /recovered/.../docker-compose.yml up -d
```

The compose file names the exact image version that was running, so the
application comes back at the version its data expects.

## 5. Reload a database

Database exports are plain SQL, readable and loadable without any of our
software:

```
zcat <name>-<date>.sql.gz | psql -U postgres
```

They were written with instructions to replace what is already there, so
loading one into an empty database is safe. Use these only if the
application's own data directory did not survive; loading an export over
newer data will overwrite it.

## 6. Rebuilding the whole server

Install the version recorded in step 3 on a fresh machine, then use its
recovery process with your backup location and password. You only ever
need those two things: where the backup lives, and the password that
opens it. Keep both somewhere separate from the server itself.

## If you get stuck

Your data is not locked behind anything. If a step above does not work,
the files are still plain files, and someone comfortable with Docker and
PostgreSQL can work from them directly.
